Every release used to end with someone opening the staging site and clicking through a checklist by hand: does every page have a title, do the internal links resolve, does the RSS feed still parse. It worked, and it was also the first thing that got skipped when a release was late.

## Turning the checklist into code

The checklist itself was already precise enough to be a spec. We rewrote it as a test suite that runs against the built output — no browser, no live server, just the static files on disk inspected with a DOM parser:

- Every internal link resolves to a real file
- Every image has alt text
- The RSS feed item count matches the number of published posts
- No draft content ships in the production build

None of this requires a browser. A parser and a filesystem are enough to catch the class of bug that used to need a human with a checklist.

## What it caught that we wouldn't have

The suite is unforgiving about things a tired human skips on the fifth release of the week — a missing meta description, a sitemap entry pointing at a page that got renamed. Small, boring bugs that would have shipped anyway because nobody wants to be the person re-clicking every link for the third time that day.

## What it still can't do

It has no opinion on whether the page looks good. Spacing, type scale, whether a headline wraps awkwardly on a small screen — that's still a person looking at a real browser. We didn't eliminate manual review; we shrank it down to the one thing a person is actually good at judging.
